verdict: safe to land. no correctness defect in the code half; all eight findings are prose.

what the reviewer re-derived rather than took. all 13 entry points of the deleted module,
checked at db6fe8d^ with comments and string literals masked: eight are callerless (_begin,
_unit, _hills, _end, _caster, _locate, _apply, _note_casters), and the five that are not return
constants. `s_live = 1` and `s_pubHave = 1` each occur exactly once, inside _begin and _end,
both callerless -- so _live() and _handover() returned 0 for the life of every process. it
widened past the helper's scope to rule out non-C reach: no *.cpp, *.def, *.rc or Makefile
reference; exports.def exports no tagpu_shadow_*; no GetProcAddress or function-pointer table.
it also ran the function-inventory check this repo learned to run after a scripted deletion
swallowed a neighbour: top-level declarators in all four edited .c files are identical to main
except the one added `static int shadow_handover`.

it confirmed the three claims the landing rests on. (a) `s_pub.shadowOn = 0` is equivalent:
both publish functions open with memset(&s_pub, 0, sizeof s_pub) and, after the edit, exactly
one write to any shadow field remains in each file -- the new constant. zero-vs-stale is
unreachable because the memset precedes every publish, and every consumer reads shadow state
only under shadowOn. (b) nothing is lost silently: TAGPU_SHADOWU had no user outside the
deleted file; TAGPU_SHADOWHAND's new home is self-sufficient for size_t through
tagpu_vk_pass.h; no file depended on the transitive tagpu_fx.h accidentally. (c) the stub drops
no side effect: s_liveHave/s_res clearing, the ST_REFUSED test, the slot bounds test and
mesh_slot_done all run before the hand-over check, and the old handover's only side effect fired
on a success path that never executed. no new warnings; no -Werror.

it independently reproduced every published figure: gl-sites 165/234 at HEAD, 87/88 for the
deleted file, 165 + 87 = 252; eight of thirteen callerless; tagpu_shadow_[a-z_]+ masked-matches
0 at HEAD; the five #include "opengl_utils.h" directives at db6fe8d^; dd.c:1908-1931 landing
every GL spelling on vk_render_main; s_hiHave written once, at tagpu_hires_draw.c:611, inside
the callerless tagpu_hires_depth.

THE FINDING WORTH THE LANDING (F6, MEDIUM). D2 orphaned `tagpu_terr_hills_draw`:
`tagpu_shadow_hills` was its only caller, masked counts 3 -> 2 across the commit. tagpu_terr.h
still said "its only call site is tagpu_shadow_hills, which is itself called from nowhere",
which after D2 is a sentence about a function that does not exist. the consequence: that
function is the ONLY reader of the heightfield CPU mirror s_hMeshV/s_hMeshI -- 19.3 MB on Two
Continents by build_hills' own arithmetic (tagpu_terr.c:354), built unconditionally -- so the
memory is now provably unread. not a regression, since a callerless caller read nothing either,
but 2.80 is the survey of what is already dark and had missed the one thing D2 itself darkened.
recorded there and at the function's own declaration, and named as the next deletion.
TAGPU_TERRHILLS likewise has no consumer outside its header.

THE FINDING AT MY EXPENSE (F1, MEDIUM). the paragraph defending raw line numbers -- on the
grounds that `git show` takes a line number -- gave the numbers for the wrong revision. it named
db6fe8d^ and cited 403 and 531, which are 6a34c3e's; D1's GLSL lift had taken 25 lines off the
top of the file. the correct pair is 378 and 506, read out of that revision to check. naming a
revision does not make a line number safe; it only narrows which tree the check runs against,
and the check still has to be run. third citation drift in this gate.

the other six, all one-line prose corrections, all verified first: "nine live call sites" is
eight and the enumeration itself summed to eight; oglu_load_dll has eighteen references outside
opengl_utils.c, seventeen comments and one prototype, not "every reference ... seventeen";
tagpu_native.c:3606 is 3607 in the plan's copy; tagpu_vk_shadow.h -- the header chosen as
